# Job: spec deep review R5-fw-transport-session (read-only diagnosis, report only)

You are a codex sub-agent under a Claude supervisor. Model gpt-6-astra, reasoning xhigh. Cap 3 h.
Read `AGENTS.md` (root) §2/§3/§5 and `doc/plan/c016-worklog/spec-review/README.ko.md` first — the README
fixes the review criteria, the findings template and the job split; follow it exactly.

## Hard rules
- READ-ONLY. Do not modify, create, move or delete any file except your one report file
  `doc/plan/c016-worklog/spec-review/R5-fw-transport-session-summary.md`. Never touch `core/**`, `bindings/**`,
  `framework/**`, any `doc/spec/**`, `framework/doc/**`, `doc/site/**`. Do not run builds, tests,
  benchmarks or git commands that change state (`git diff`/`git log`/`git grep` are fine). Do not commit.
- Evidence, not opinion: every finding cites spec `file:line` for *every* place the rule is stated and
  implementation `file:line` per language you examined. If you could not verify a language, say so.
- One finding = one cause. Do not propose mitigations (timeouts, retries, extra pollers, catch-alls).
  Every proposal is "N rules → M rules" with the draft sentence of the merged rule.
- Mark `행동 변경: 있음` whenever an application could observe a difference; the supervisor schedules
  those for 0.18.0 and applies only `없음` items now — be precise.
- Do not rewrite spec text at length; a proposal is one merged sentence plus where it lives.

## Scope (read all of it)
`framework/doc/framework/common/spec/server/02-channel-transport/*.ko.md` (topology, messaging, client-server channel, listener identity, transport liveness, wire protocol) and `04-session/*.ko.md` (stream session, session-actor binding) (≈5,000 lines)

Implementation evidence: `framework/languages/{cpp,dotnet,java,node,kotlin}/**` mesh/channel/session runtimes; lower-layer guarantees in `bindings/doc/spec/README.ko.md` and `core/doc/spec/core/socket/README.ko.md` (§4 admission, D-090/D-092/D-098) — read the relevant Core/binding clauses so you can tell re-verification from genuine framework rules

## What to look for in this scope
- Highest-value target: framework-level connection/liveness/reconnect logic that re-verifies Core guarantees. Core now publishes DISCONNECTED at peer termination (D-092), fails submit-time-terminated requests with NOT_CONNECTED at once (D-090), releases endpoints before unbind/close return (D-098), and decides RID duplicates by REJECT/HANDOVER only (D-094). Find framework text (and code) that still: waits a timer to "confirm" termination, keeps a second readiness state machine, re-tries admission on its own schedule, or re-validates RID uniqueness. Each is `lower-layer-reverification`; quantify the rule/timer/state removed.
- Peer admission is described in `01-channel-topology` (duplicate peer rule), `03-mesh-node` §7.1 (R6's file — cross-reference only), `03-client-server-channel` (generations/re-admission) and `05-transport-liveness` §5/§6. Is "when is a peer admitted/ready" owned by one document? (`scattered-control`)
- ClientServer: readiness deadline cap "min(request timeout, 5 s)" and reconnection generations — needed, or a mitigation-shaped rule?
- Session (`04-session`): stream session close/disconnect ownership (node `6e14b3e8fe` fixed "close of already-gone session must complete"): is the single-claim DISCONNECTED rule (Core `47a8f4329b`) mirrored as one framework rule?
- Wire protocol (`06-wire-protocol`): contract bytes vs codec narrative separation (§4.4).

## Quality bar
`doc/principal/documentation/spec-writing-guide.ko.md` §1 (reader questions), §2.4/§4.4 (bold rule +
reason; contract vs implementation narrative; `**언어별 재량**` only with the parity reason), §4.3 (one
layer per rule), §9.3 (verification section = interface observations only). Campaign rules:
`doc/plan/c016-worklog/decisions.ko.md` D-090…D-101 are the freshest decisions; the supervisor's
principles are "one owner per rule", "simple = fewer rules", "no mitigations".

